<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/inventions?focus=kite">Inventions &amp; Discoveries</a> · Transport and navigation</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kite</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">by 549</span> · <a href="/centuries/6th-century">6th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">A kite is a tethered craft heavier than air whose wing surfaces react against the wind to give lift. It was first made in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, where kites of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk">silk</a> and bamboo, and of paper after its invention in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/han-dynasty">Han dynasty</a>, served to measure distances, test the wind, lift men and signal in war; by 549 CE a kite carried a message for a rescue mission. Kites spread to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, where the fighter kite developed, and across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polynesia">Polynesia</a>, but reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> late: Konrad Kyeser described dragon kites about 1400. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benjamin-franklin">Benjamin Franklin</a> published an account of a kite experiment on lightning in 1752, and kites served the research of the Wright brothers toward the first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aeroplane">airplane</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kite.jpg" alt="Kite" width="330" height="317" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Francisco Goya · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:La_cometa.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> the kite has been claimed as the invention of the 5th-century BCE philosophers Mozi and Lu Ban, who flew wooden kites called muyuan. After paper was invented in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/han-dynasty">Han dynasty</a>, paper kites, called zhiyuan, became popular. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk">Silk</a> for the sail, fine and strong silk for the line and resilient bamboo for the frame were all readily available. By the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tang-dynasty">Tang dynasty</a>, bamboo flutes, whistles and ribbons were attached to kites to make sound, a forerunner of the modern fengzheng, named for that sound. The earliest known Chinese kites were flat and often rectangular, and later tailless kites carried a stabilising bowline. They were decorated with mythological motifs and figures.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>Ancient Chinese texts describe kites used for measuring distances, testing the wind, lifting men, signalling and communication in military operations. By 549 a kite was used to carry a message for a rescue mission. During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song dynasty</a> the Fire Crow, a kite carrying incendiary powder, was made as a weapon. According to the Samguk Sagi, the Korean general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kim-yu-sin">Kim Yu-sin</a> of Silla rallied his troops in 647 with flaming kites that also frightened the enemy.</p><p>In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> the kite evolved into the fighter kite, the patang, and thousands are flown every year at festivals such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/makar-sankranti">Makar Sankranti</a>. Kites were known throughout <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polynesia">Polynesia</a> as far as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-zealand">New Zealand</a>, probably carried from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> with the people. Polynesian kites of cloth and wood in human form were used in religious ceremonies to send prayers to the gods.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>Kites reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> late, although the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> knew windsock-like banners. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marco-polo">Marco Polo</a> brought stories of them in the late 13th century, and sailors brought kites back from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> and Malaysia in the 16th and 17th centuries. Konrad Kyeser described dragon kites in Bellifortis about 1400. At first they were regarded as curiosities, but by the 18th and 19th centuries they served scientific research. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benjamin-franklin">Benjamin Franklin</a> published an account of a kite experiment in 1752 to show that lightning was electrical, and kites aided the Wright brothers' research toward the first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aeroplane">airplane</a>. The years from 1860 to about 1910 became the European golden age of kiting. Powered aircraft then reduced interest in them.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Kite” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="inv-entry"><h2 class="enc-section-title">On the inventions tree</h2><dl class="inv-entry-facts"><div><dt>First known</dt><dd>by 549</dd></div><div><dt>Where</dt><dd>China</dd></div></dl><p class="inv-entry-note"><span class="inv-k">Note</span>The kite has been claimed as the invention of the 5th-century BCE Chinese philosophers Mozi and Lu Ban, who are said to have flown wooden kites called muyuan.</p><div class="inv-entry-rel"><span class="inv-k">Built on</span><a class="inv-chip" href="/encyclopedia/sericulture">Silk<span>c. 3500 BCE</span></a><a class="inv-chip" href="/encyclopedia/papermaking">Papermaking<span>179–141 BCE</span></a></div><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/inventions?focus=kite">See it on the inventions tree &rarr;</a></p></section>
